1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
You might think about, what would some examples of this be?

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Well, let's see.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Of course, any real world example, you may always dispute.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,000
One that comes to my mind is, if there is a person much bigger, tougher than you,

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
and they're leaving you alone, you go right up to them and you offer to fight them.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,000
There's no one around or anything.

7
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
It's like, look, either this person is as big a stuff as they seem,

8
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,000
in which case they're going to crush you,

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
or they weren't, in which case you would pick to fight with a total stranger for nothing.

10
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,000
So just don't do it.

11
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Now you might say, that's more complicated than that maybe is,

12
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,000
but anyway, that's the idea of the Strictly Dominant Strategy,

13
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:51,000
something that is so stupid that it's never a good idea, no matter what.

14
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
So, you may think of other examples from your own life.

15
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Like someone deliberately going and assaulting a person upon whose good will they rely.

16
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Why would you do that?

17
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Why would you say something like that to that person?

18
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Either they'll be really offended,

19
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,000
or at least you'll have missed an opportunity to have said something that would be constructive.

20
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,000
So either way, terrible idea.

21
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:23,000
So anyway, the strategy that is worse for you no matter what your opponent does, play it.

22
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,000
And then, if elimination of Strictly Dominant Strategies leaves you with a single equilibrium,

23
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,000
so it doesn't have to,

24
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,000
but if it so happens that once you delete everything that is never a good idea,

25
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,000
you're left with only one thing,

26
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
then you go out on a limb and say,

27
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
you know what I'm going to predict happens?

28
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:42,000
The one thing left.

29
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,000
That's what I'm going to predict.

30
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:46,000
And that is what the solution concept is like.

31
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,000
And we call that a game that is dominant solvable.

32
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,000
One where you can simply delete stuff that would always be a dumb idea,

33
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,000
and once you've done all those deletions, you're left with one thing,

34
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,000
and then you're back down to solvable.

35
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:02,000
And the classic example of this is the Prisoner's Lemma.

36
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,000
So you've all seen the Prisoner's Lemma at one point.

37
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,000
The classic version has two prisoners.

38
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
They both get caught, but they're separated,

39
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
and each one is told, look, either,

40
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
so if you confess, we will give you a reduced sentence.

41
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:22,000
But there's only one plea bargain on the table here.

42
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
So if your accomplice goes and confesses first,

43
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,000
then he gets the good deal and you get the crummy deal.

44
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,000
And if neither of you confesses, then you both get a light sentence.

45
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,000
But of course if you both confess, then ha ha,

46
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,000
then you're both going to get a pretty bad sentence.

47
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,000
The worst one, though, is if you don't confess and your opponent does,

48
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,000
then you get the really harsh punishment.

49
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
So that's what the Prisoner's Lemma is all about.

50
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,000
And the standard setup of the Prisoner's Lemma is people say, well, look,

51
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
all right, well, can I control what my accomplice in the other room does?

52
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
No. All right, so two possibilities.

53
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
One is that he doesn't go and betray me.

54
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,000
In this case, I could go and get off scot-free if I betray him,

55
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,000
so that seems like a good idea.

56
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
The other one is, well, maybe he is going to betray me.

57
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,000
In that case, if I betray him, then at least I don't get the really bad sentence.

58
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,000
So that means that whether your opponent is betraying you

59
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:18,000
or cooperating with you, you are always better off strictly by betraying him.

60
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,000
That is the case.

61
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,000
And then the nice thing about the Prisoner's Lemma is your accomplice

62
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
has exactly the same incentives.

63
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,000
So the other player has exactly the same incentives.

64
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:30,000
So since it's strictly dominant for you to never cooperate with him,

65
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
it's strictly dominant for him to never cooperate with you,

66
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:35,000
and the game is dominant-solvable,

67
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:39,000
and the dominant-solvable result is no one ever cooperates with anyone.

68
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,000
And there is actually an economic detective model called Murder of the Martian

69
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,000
you may have heard about, written by Ken Elzinga from UVA

70
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,000
under the pseudonym Marshall Jevons.

71
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
And in the detective novel, there's a guy who goes and confesses to a crime

72
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
for which he's innocent, and the economist comes along and says,

73
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:00,000
well, why would he confess if he's innocent?

74
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,000
And the economist says, have you ever heard of the Prisoner's Lemma?

75
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Why don't we go explain?

76
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Of course, when you read the book, it's like, well, if that's true,

77
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,000
then why does anyone believe confessions ever?

78
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:14,000
If everyone always confesses whether they're innocent or guilty,

79
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,000
why does anybody think that confessions prove anything?

80
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:22,000
And I don't think that's really resolved in the novel.

81
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:28,000
If you read more widely, it's like if you read Roman philosopher Cicero,

82
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:34,000
he even has a reply to a piece in defense of torturing confessions out of people.

83
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,000
And if you think about this, it's like, look, if you torture someone,

84
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,000
they will confess whether they're innocent or guilty,

85
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:44,000
so actually torture is a terrible way of learning about who's guilty, right?

86
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:48,000
And then Cicero says, well, it depends upon how you torture them.

87
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
It's like, really? Right? And remember, he's a really smart guy.

88
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,000
So he says, look, if you torture them just to say, yes, I did it,

89
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:58,000
then of course torture is an idiotic way of finding out, of solving crimes.

90
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:01,000
But suppose you torture them until they tell you something

91
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,000
they couldn't possibly know unless they were guilty.

92
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Ha ha! All right.

93
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:10,000
You torture them until they tell you where the murder weapon is.

94
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:13,000
That actually, you learn something from that torture.

95
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:16,000
And of course, it made them, wow, he died. I guess he was innocent.

96
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,000
Or, it's like, wow, the third finger came off,

97
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:22,000
and then he told us where the murder weapon was. See?

98
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
It was justified.

99
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,000
So this is what a smart person with a very different worldview from our own

100
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
had to say about the strategic situation.

101
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,000
I was like, I read it, and I was like, wow, this guy's pretty smart, actually.

102
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
All right, so at least he's saying something much better

103
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:40,000
than most modern defenders of torture would say.

104
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
It's a winner.

105
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
All right, so that's the prisoner's lemma.

106
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:47,000
It's a classic example of a dominant, solvable game.

107
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:49,000
Questions?

108
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,000
Happy? Right? Yes?

109
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
I guess I'm going through something.

